 s=importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('fresh_builder',W/'experiments/clock-mlp27-input/prepare.py');m=importlib.util.module_from_spec(s);s.loader.exec_module(m);m.R=R
 m.PROSE=[
 ('discovery','rain_gauge','Explain how to use a simple rain gauge.','A rain gauge collects falling water in a container with a marked scale. The observer places it on level ground away from a roof or overhanging branch. After a shower, she reads the water level at eye height and records the value with the date. She then empties the container so that the next measurement covers a new period. Leaving old water inside would combine two separate intervals. The measurement describes the depth of rain at that location rather than the total volume falling over the whole garden.'),
 ('discovery','pulley_rope','Explain what a fixed pulley changes when lifting a small load.','A fixed pulley guides a rope over a wheel attached above the load. Pulling one end down raises the object at the other end. The pulley changes the direction of the pull and can make the task more convenient. It does not remove the need to support the weight. The rope must fit securely in the groove of the wheel. A damaged attachment is checked before any object is raised. Moving the rope slowly helps the operator notice a snag. After the object rests on a support, tension can be released safely.'),
 ('confirmation','hand_sieve','Describe separating small stones from loose soil.','The gardener places a hand sieve over a wide tray and adds a small amount of dry soil. Gentle movement lets the smaller particles pass through the mesh. Larger stones and roots remain above it. Filling the sieve too deeply makes separation slower because fine soil cannot easily reach the openings. The gardener removes retained material before adding another portion. A torn mesh would admit larger pieces and change the result. The method separates particles mainly by their size rather than by their color or weight.'),
 ('confirmation','door_mat','Describe cleaning a small outdoor mat.','The resident lifts the mat and brushes loose grit from the surface underneath it. She shakes the mat where the dust will not blow into the doorway. Mud is allowed to dry before it is brushed out of the coarse fibers. If washing is suitable for the material, the mat is rinsed and left to drain. It should dry before being returned to the threshold. Trapped water can wet the floor and make the surface slippery. Cleaning the mat and the area below it keeps debris from being carried indoors.'),
 ('confirmation','glass_jar','Describe preparing a glass jar for storing dry beans.','The cook checks that the empty jar has no crack around its rim. She washes the jar and lid and leaves both to dry completely. Dry beans are added only when no moisture remains inside. A label records the contents and the date so the jar can be identified without opening it. The lid is closed securely but is not forced if its thread does not line up. The filled jar is kept in a cool cupboard away from direct sunlight. A clean container protects the beans without changing their quantity.'),
 ('confirmation','paper_envelope','Describe addressing and closing a paper envelope.','The writer folds the letter so that it fits without stretching the envelope. She places the recipient address clearly on the front and checks that the postal code agrees with the destination. A return address is placed where it can be distinguished from the recipient address. Before closing the flap, she checks that the intended pages are inside. The envelope is sealed along its prepared edge rather than joined with a bulky knot of tape. Keeping the writing legible helps the postal service route the letter.')]
 m.MATH=[('discovery','bead_boxes','Explain a simple bead inventory calculation.','There are eight boxes with fifteen beads in each box. Multiplying eight by fifteen gives one hundred and twenty beads. A worker takes twenty six beads for a bracelet and thirty four for another item. The two withdrawals total sixty beads, so sixty remain. A check adds the remaining sixty to twenty six and thirty four. The result is one hundred and twenty again. The calculation counts all beads equally without distinguishing their colors. No new beads arrive between the withdrawals. The answer describes stock remaining after both items have been made.'),('confirmation','fence_posts','Explain how many fence posts remain after two repairs.','A shed contains seventy five fence posts. The first repair uses eighteen posts and leaves fifty seven. A second repair uses twenty three, leaving thirty four. Combining the two repairs gives forty one used posts. Adding forty one and thirty four gives the original seventy five. All posts are counted as whole items, and none are cut into extra posts. The two repairs happen before any replacement delivery. The final stock count is therefore thirty four. Listing each withdrawal separately helps identify a subtraction error if the counts disagree.'),('confirmation','tram_fares','Explain the cost of several identical tram tickets.','Five tram tickets cost three units each. Multiplying five by three gives fifteen units. A traveler pays with twenty units and receives five units in change. The change can be checked by adding it to the ticket cost. Five plus fifteen equals twenty. All tickets have the same price and no discount or additional fee is included. The number of tickets must not be confused with the total amount paid. One ticket costs three units, while the complete group costs fifteen. The calculation uses only the stated prices.')]
 m.CODE=[('discovery','join_labels','Write a function copying each supplied label as a string.','copy_label_strings','items','result.append(str(item))','[]'),('confirmation','count_flags','Write a function counting true values.','count_true_flags','flags','result += int(bool(item))','0'),('confirmation','last_match','Write a function remembering the last positive number.','last_positive','values','if item > 0:\n            result = item','0')]
